{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_1","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc 'cat .agents/skills/code-review-pr/SKILL.md; cat collab/1792862294000-qa-engineer-datomic-q-phase4a-review.glm-5.3.findings.md; cat collab/1792862294000-qa-engineer-datomic-q-phase4a-fixes-confirm.glm-5.3.findings.md'","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_1","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc 'cat .agents/skills/code-review-pr/SKILL.md; cat collab/1792862294000-qa-engineer-datomic-q-phase4a-review.glm-5.3.findings.md; cat collab/1792862294000-qa-engineer-datomic-q-phase4a-fixes-confirm.glm-5.3.findings.md'","aggregated_output":"---\nname: code-review-pr\ndescription: >-\n  Performs comprehensive, high-quality code reviews on Git pull requests, branches, commit ranges, or local working tree diffs. Analyzes code correctness, security, architecture, test coverage, and performance using automated diff analysis and structured review checklists.\n---\n\n# Code Review PR Skill\n\n## Overview\n\nThe `code-review-pr` skill provides an end-to-end framework for conducting rigorous, actionable code reviews on Git pull requests, feature branches, commit ranges, or working tree diffs. It combines automated diff scanning via `scripts/pr_reviewer.py` with structured review checklists across security, correctness, architecture, performance, and testing.\n\nUse this skill whenever asked to:\n- Review a pull request or feature branch (`git diff main...HEAD`).\n- Review recent commits (`git diff HEAD~1` or commit range).\n- Review uncommitted local changes (`git diff` or staged changes).\n- Perform a security, quality, or architecture audit of proposed code changes.\n\n---\n\n## Quick Start\n\nRun the automated PR reviewer script to generate an initial risk assessment, churn metrics, and automated smell scan:\n\n```bash\n# 1. Review branch changes against main (Default PR review)\npython3 .agents/skills/code-review-pr/scripts/pr_reviewer.py analyze --target main...HEAD\n\n# 2. Review last commit\npython3 .agents/skills/code-review-pr/scripts/pr_reviewer.py analyze --target HEAD~1\n\n# 3. Review uncommitted working tree changes\npython3 .agents/skills/code-review-pr/scripts/pr_reviewer.py analyze --target working\n\n# 4. Save review report to file\npython3 .agents/skills/code-review-pr/scripts/pr_reviewer.py analyze --target main...HEAD --output review_report.md\n```\n\n---\n\n## Code Review Workflow\n\nFollow this step-by-step process when executing a code review:\n\n### Step 1: Run Automated Diff Analysis\nExecute `scripts/pr_reviewer.py analyze` for the target Git ref/range. Review the output to determine:\n- Total lines added/deleted and file churn distribution.\n- High-risk modified files (e.g., Security/Auth, Database/Schema, Infrastructure, API Contracts).\n- Test-to-source additions ratio.\n- Automated static findings (hardcoded secrets, debug leftovers, swallowed exceptions, SQL injection risks).\n\n### Step 2: Deep-Dive Code Inspection\nInspect the diffs for high-risk files using `git diff <target> -- <path>` or `view_file`. Focus on:\n1. **Logic & Edge Cases**:\n   - Boundary checks, null/nil handling, empty collections, zero division.\n   - Resource leaks (unclosed streams, file handles, DB connections).\n   - Race conditions, shared state mutation, concurrency locks.\n2. **Security & Credentials**:\n   - No hardcoded API keys, passwords, or tokens.\n   - Input validation before execution or query interpolation.\n   - Proper authorization checks on new or updated endpoints.\n3. **Architecture & Contracts**:\n   - Preserving backward compatibility on existing API functions/endpoints.\n   - Proper module separation and clean interface boundaries.\n4. **Test Coverage & Quality**:\n   - Ensuring new source logic is accompanied by unit/integration tests.\n   - Verifying tests validate actual assertions rather than just non-crashing execution.\n\n### Step 3: Reference Checklist & Severity Taxonomy\nConsult [references/review_checklist.md](references/review_checklist.md) to classify findings:\n- **P0 (Critical / Blocker)**: Security breaches, data loss risks, severe crashes, hardcoded credentials.\n- **P1 (High / Must Address)**: Logic bugs, broken API contracts, swallowed exceptions, missing critical tests.\n- **P2 (Medium / Recommended)**: Performance bottlenecks, code duplication, missing parameter validation.\n- **P3 (Low / Suggestion)**: Minor formatting, naming improvements, leftover comments/TODOs.\n\n### Step 4: Formulate Structured Review Report\nPresent findings to the user using the following template:\n\n```markdown\n# 🔍 Code Review Report\n\n## Executive Summary\n- **Target:** `<git-ref>`\n- **Verdict:** `[ APPROVED | REQUEST CHANGES | COMMENT ]`\n- **Overall Risk:** `[ LOW | MEDIUM | HIGH ]`\n- **Metrics:** `X files changed, +Y / -Z lines`\n\n## 🚨 Critical Findings (P0 / P1)\n- **[Severity]** `path/to/file.ext:L123` - Short issue description.\n  ```diff\n  - problematic code\n  + suggested fix\n  ```\n\n## 💡 Improvements & Suggestions (P2 / P3)\n- **[Severity]** `path/to/file.ext` - Recommendation details.\n\n## 🧪 Test Coverage & Quality Assessment\n- Evaluation of test ratio and edge case coverage.\n\n## 🌟 Positive Highlights\n- Clean implementations, thorough documentation, or well-structured refactoring.\n```\n\n---\n\n## Utility Script Reference\n\nThe bundled helper script `scripts/pr_reviewer.py` supports the following options:\n\n| Parameter | Type | Default | Description |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| `--target` | String | `main...HEAD` | Git range/ref (`main...HEAD`, `HEAD~1`, `staged`, `working`) |\n| `--output` | String | None | File path to write report output |\n| `--format` | String | `markdown` | Output format (`markdown` or `json`) |\n| `--repo-dir` | String | Current Dir | Path to the target Git repository |\n\n---\n\n## Common Review Pitfalls\n\n1. **Nit Tunnel Vision**: Focusing heavily on formatting or naming while missing underlying logic bugs or edge-case crashes.\n2. **Ignoring Test Ratio**: Approving large source code changes without verifying corresponding test coverage additions.\n3. **Swallowing Symptoms**: Suggesting `try/except` wrappers around failing code rather than addressing root cause invalid inputs or broken state.\nCoding-Agent: glm · Session-ID: 09098796-22e1-41cf-b24b-f98b489eef39 · Phase 4a adversarial review\n## Checklist verdicts\n1. **ONE evaluator — PASS.** `q` is literally `(second (apply q-with-state exec/unbounded nil query inputs))` (query.cljc:2148–2149); zero occurrences of a non-star `eval-where`; the old evaluator is gone. Public signatures and result-value shape unchanged (`{:dao.space.query/result …, :spec …, :return-map-*}`); `collect`, `datoms`, `match`, `pull/pull-many` intact; `realize-db-value!` preserved for public consumers under the unbounded policy. `test/dao/space/query_test.cljc` is not in the modified set and ran **72/282 green** under the rewritten kernel.\n2. **State threading — PASS.** No atom/volatile/dynamic-var/set! in any of the three src files (grep clean; the only hits are docstring text). The kernel is CPS with immutable `ctx` (lexical scope: fns, active-rule path, driver) vs `st` (progress) — scope restoration on rule return is structural (caller's ctx is never mutated), while the body's returned `st` continues; `each-st` pulls one item at a time and threads the sibling-returned state. Tests prove sibling-branch sharing (`exhausts? (dec both)`), nested negation/rules on one allowance, and scope-restore-without-rollback (`n2 > 2·n1`, second sibling call not blocked by the first's active key).\n3. **First-witness — PASS.** `exists-st` terminates at the first witness via `reduced`; the ruling's exact demand is tested: `first-witness-termination-in-negation` uses a fn that **throws** on any candidate ≥ 2, asserts the answer and `boom = 1` — a later candidate that would fail is never evaluated.\n4. **Forcing discipline — PASS.** No lazy chain captures stale state (all loops are explicit `each-st`/`recur` folds observing per item); chunked realization can't bypass (items via `seq`/`next`, one at a time); `estimate-clause-cost` still truthiness-only with the planning-never-forces comment; `plan-where` shape unchanged. On the \"no new regression added\" question: **existing coverage is sufficient** — `query_test.cljc` (unmodified, green under the new kernel) asserts actual storage `gets ≤ 4` (\"strictly fewer gets than reading full index\") and `≤ 15` (\"the planner must not drain the source\") over opened published indexes for unbounded calls, which is precisely the ruling's selective-read regression requirement.\n5. **Admission fail-closed — PASS, probed firsthand.** `source-admissible?` is purely structural (vector? on the relation slot — a delay fails; `:published` → false; views recurse into `:source`), never forces. Tests cover direct/wrapped/double-wrapped published, deferred rows, view-over-deferred, warmed cache, and mixed sources naming the **unused** source's variable (`$b`), across timeout-only/steps-only/both, asserting zero reads. My own probes: warmed published cache refused `:unsupported/:bounded-source-unqualified/\"$\"` with 0 reads under both bound kinds; deferred relation same; unbounded deferred still works.\n6. **Expiry semantics — PASS.** `>=` at every observation (execution.cljc:148; 499/500/501 boundary test); terminal states fully absorbing — whole-state equality after later checkpoints (machinery correction 1, tested); `read-step` returns `::not-read`/`::discarded` structurally and the integrated host-fn test proves post-expiry values are never interpreted (correction 2); the deadline is minted before parsing (counter-clock test proves the clock read precedes the parse failure); kernel `spend`/`spend-n`/`datoms-st` follow observe→charge→perform→observe; adapter `advance` participates from parse (string-length cost) through output (row count) to `finish`.\n7. **Errors — PASS.** `:binding` is gone from producer ex-data — probed firsthand: the not-join unknown-fn path now carries only `(:fn :clause)` (bounded syntax fragments); the four remaining `:binding-form` hits are shape descriptors, not caller bindings. Exhaustion is terminal data in the kernel, translated only at the adapter to `:timeout`/`:step-budget-exhausted`, both `:cognitect.anomalies/interrupted`, minimal keys, no source handles. `:rule-depth-limit` maps to `:unsupported` with `{:reason}`.\n8. **Machinery corrections — PASS, both landed and tested.** `checkpoint` checks terminality first and tests assert whole-state identity after subsequent checkpoints/charges/finish; `read-step`'s discard is enforced at the primitive, in `run-reads`, and at the integrated level.\n9. **Open items — adjudicated.** (a) *rule-depth 200*: contract-conformant for 4a — loud, bounded-only, data-independent, tested (including small-cap-exhausts-first precedence), and the ruling's \"loops or explicit frames where necessary\" authorizes rather than mandates frames; but it narrows the bounded qualified domain (>200 nesting refused even with budget), so the implementer is right to escalate — see finding 1. (b) *static or/or-join validation*: conformant (\"reject statically when recognizable\"); strictness-increasing only, legacy 72 + downstream green; accepted. (c) *indivisible ops*: documented in `step-units`/docstrings, and the host-fn case is in fact integration-tested; accepted. (d) *JVM-only clock smoke*: gap — see finding 2. (e) *no corpus timeout pins*: acceptable for 4a (corpus frozen, harness runs core q unbounded), but pending reasons are now stale — finding 3.\n10. **Portability — PARTIAL.** Cljd-guards verified in source on the two host-specific tests (`#?(:cljd nil :clj …)` ×3 including the machinery smoke); no host-conditional domain reduction beyond the documented Dart wall-clock fallback (which is documented in both the ns docstring and `default-now-ms`, per contract). Three-host gate (408/402/402) is implementer-claimed, not reproduced in this seat — finding 4.\n## Findings\nP3 | src/cljc/dao/space/query.cljc:1616-1623 | `max-bounded-rule-depth 200` narrows the bounded qualified domain (legitimate deep rule nesting is refused even with sufficient budget); the ruling leaves frames-vs-limit to the architect and the implementer has flagged it | record the limit in the contract/errata as part of the bounded profile and get the architect's ratification (or an explicit frame-based design in a later slice) before Phase 5 qualification\nP3 | test/dao/space/query/execution_test.cljc:229-239, datomic_execution_test.cljc:208-217 | real-clock smokes are JVM-only (two of them); the ruling asks for \"host clock smoke coverage\" and the contract allows one per host — the Node `performance.now` and Dart `DateTime.now` fallback paths are never smoke-exercised | add one generously-margined real-clock smoke on the Node and Dart lanes, or record an architect-accepted exemption (deterministic fake-clock tests do run on all three hosts)\nP3 | test/resources/dao/space/datomic-q-v1.json (opts.timeout cases) | the corpus's pending reasons (\"refused until the execution-bounds machinery lands\") are stale after 4a: `:timeout` now works bounded-only over materialized sources; no corpus case pins the new surface or the admission refusal | one reviewed corpus/errata update recording the Phase 4a timeout/max-steps surface (re-digest per procedure); full timeout oracle cases belong to Phase 5\nP3 | review evidence | the three-host subsystem gate (408/402/402) and downstream JVM gate (304/39,207 incl. dht+transact) are implementer-claimed and not reproduced in this review seat; the ruling makes both landing conditions | attach the orchestrator's or CI's three-host and downstream run logs to the landing record\nP3 | src/cljc/dao/space/query.cljc:490-524 (pre-existing, surfaced by probing) | `{:dao.space.query/relation <delay of a non-sequence>}` yields an empty result instead of refusing (realize-st checks only `map?` + key presence); bounded calls are safe (admission requires `vector?`), only unbounded calls see it, and it predates this slice | have `realize-st` refuse when the relation slot is neither a delay of/over tuples nor sequential (route with the next core slice; not a 4a regression)\n## Commands run (with outcomes)\n- Four suites firsthand, all exit 0, 0 failures: `query-test` (72), `datomic-test` (29), `execution-test` (35), `datomic-execution-test` (17) = **153 tests** matching the orchestrator's reproduction; conformance suite green (exit 0).\n- kondo over all six changed/new files + examples: **0 errors, 0 warnings**.\n- Probes via `-M:oracle -e`: warmed published cache (both bound kinds) → `:unsupported/:bounded-source-unqualified/\"$\"`, **0 reads**; deferred relation refused, 0 reads; unbounded deferred still admitted; static or-validation throws on legacy q (\"All branches of or must bind the same set of variables\"); not-join ex-data = `(:fn :clause)`.\n- Read-only greps: no mutable state in src; no `:binding` in ex-data; no second evaluator; published selective-read regressions located in the unmodified core suite (gets ≤ 4 / ≤ 15); contract amendments verified transcribed (status header, `0 → 1 → 4a → 4b → 2 → 3 → 5`, 4a/4b rows, `max-steps` row); `git diff --stat` scoped the rewrite; ble/Flutter files untouched.\n- Not reproduced in this seat (implementer-claimed, flagged in finding 4): three-host gate 408/402/402, downstream 304/39,207, node-discovery.\n**Verdict: ENDORSE WITH CHANGES** — the Route A rewrite is a faithful, well-tested implementation of the r3 ruling (one state-passing kernel, fail-closed admission with zero reads, exact `>=` boundaries, absorbing terminals, first-witness preserved, F6 producer fix verified in ex-data), with all four JVM suites and kondo reproduced firsthand; the five P3s are ratification/evidence/documentation items — chiefly architect sign-off on the rule-depth limit and the unverified three-host/downstream logs — none of which contradict the delivered code.\nAll five items verified resolved (read-only; every suite re-run firsthand):\n**(A) Trampoline — RESOLVED per r4.** `max-bounded-rule-depth`/`:rule-depth-limit` grep-clean (0 hits across src+tests); the trampoline landed (query.cljc:412 \"explicit execution frames\", 17 bounce/run-comp sites); the four stack-safety deftests are present exactly as described (fresh-value beyond the former ~330-level overflow, mutual ping/pong, many-bindings/deep-chains, nested-evaluation incl. witness-at-depth and the infinite-alternative first-witness case). Execution suite ran **39 tests / 246 assertions / 0 failures** firsthand. The amended contract's stack-safety paragraph is transcribed (:499–502).\n**(B) Per-host clock smoke — RESOLVED.** `real-clock-smoke` is now unguarded (datomic_execution_test.cljc:203, runs JVM+Node+Dart: adjacent-reading checks + 60s-timeout query); the JVM-only nanoTime margin test is split out and guarded (:214–216); the machinery smoke stays JVM-only as allowed.\n**(C) Corpus — RESOLVED.** `bounds.published-source-refused` exists (pending, documented-difference, E12) with the *right* honesty — \"no Client counterpart… nothing to measure against the oracle\" (it correctly does not masquerade as an oracle case); counts 82/36 confirmed by jq; digest `a4983d69…` pinned exactly 1× in the test literal + 1× in provenance; `3b20589f` survives only in the `:authored` superseded line.\n**(D) Gates — noted as orchestrator-reproduced** (clj 413/0, cljs 407/0, cljd 407/0; downstream 232/38,925 with query-test 304/39,207). My JVM-side numbers (72/282 unchanged, 29/222, 39/246, 18/172, conformance 17/41 — all 0 failures, exit 0) match the claims exactly and are consistent with the reported gate composition.\n**(E) E13 — RESOLVED.** Routes the pre-existing `realize-st` relation-slot laxness to Phase 2 explicitly, \"no code changed here and no corpus case pins it\" — the correct disposition for a non-4a regression.\n**Honest residual — accepted.** Per-step frame allocation (~18–20s execution suite) is a disclosed performance cost, not a correctness issue; pattern parsing and pull nesting remain bounded recursion over the *query text's* own depth, which is finite by construction and (for string queries) pre-bounded by the 64-depth/100k scanner. No hidden unbounded stack remains in kernel evaluation.\n**Verdict: ENDORSE** — Phase 4a is cleared to land: the r4 trampoline replaces the depth heuristic with structured exhaustion at constant host stack, all five suites reproduce exactly, both landing-condition gates are reproduced, and the residual costs are disclosed and bounded.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
