{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_2","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"cat src/cljc/dao/space/query/execution.cljc; sed -n '390,650p' src/cljc/dao/space/query.cljc\"","aggregated_output":"(ns dao.space.query.execution\n  \"Generic bounded-execution machinery for dao.space query\n   (docs/design/dao.space.query.datomic-compat.md, Normative execution\n   bounds). It names no Client option and no query clause.\n\n   One invocation owns one IMMUTABLE state: progress, remaining work\n   allowance, deadline data, phase. Every operation takes a state and returns\n   a new state; nothing here is an atom, volatile, dynamic var, callback or\n   namespace-level clock. A caller that forks the same state into two\n   branches has not threaded it: each step must receive the state the prior\n   step returned.\n\n   Time enters only through an explicit driver (`IDriver`): a synchronous\n   observation the driver takes when a checkpoint asks for one. The driver is\n   never registered and never stored in the state; the deadline is absolute\n   data in the same unit the driver reports.\n\n   Exhaustion is a terminal DATA outcome, not an exception: once a state\n   is terminal, every later charge, observation and read returns the same\n   outcome without touching the driver (no read after observed expiry). The\n   public adapter translates the outcome to an exception.\n\n   Status (Phase 4a): the state is threaded through the one evaluation\n   kernel in `dao.space.query` (`q-with-state`) and the datomic adapter; legacy\n   `q` runs the same kernel with `unbounded`. Storage reads inside a lazy\n   B-tree range are NOT checkpointed per node fetch: bounded calls are\n   admitted only over materialized sources, and checked published-index\n   traversal is the separately reviewed Phase 4b.\")\n\n\n(defprotocol IDriver\n  (-observe [driver]\n    \"One synchronous time observation, a number in the deadline's unit.\"))\n\n\n(defrecord FnDriver [now-fn]\n  IDriver\n  (-observe [_] (now-fn)))\n\n\n(defn fn-driver\n  \"A driver over a zero-argument now-fn (monotonic where the host offers\n   one; a wall-clock fallback must be documented by the composer).\"\n  [now-fn]\n  (->FnDriver now-fn))\n\n\n(def step-units\n  \"The operation-to-unit table, as data. Work units are logical accounting,\n   not CPU instructions or elapsed time. A candidate step is one attempted\n   candidate-binding production, charged whether it is retained or rejected.\n   The other entries are the explicit units for operations that could\n   otherwise continue without attempting a binding. Opaque host functions,\n   parsing primitives and storage calls are INDIVISIBLE: one unit does not\n   preempt them.\"\n  {:candidate 1\n   :scan-advance 1\n   :rule-entry 1\n   :aggregate-input 1\n   :pull-advance 1\n   :parse-step 1\n   :output-row 1})\n\n\n(def phases\n  \"Phase boundaries observed at entry and exit.\"\n  [:parse :realize :plan :join :nested :recursion :aggregate :pull :output])\n\n\n(defn start\n  \"A fresh invocation state. `:deadline` is absolute data in the driver's\n   unit (nil: none); `:max-steps` is the work allowance (nil: unbounded, no\n   ambient default).\"\n  [{:keys [deadline max-steps]}]\n  (when-not (or (nil? max-steps) (and (integer? max-steps) (pos? max-steps)))\n    (throw (ex-info \"max-steps must be a positive integer\" {:option :max-steps})))\n  {::steps 0\n   ::remaining max-steps\n   ::deadline deadline\n   ::phase nil\n   ::outcome nil})\n\n\n(def unbounded\n  \"The explicit never-expiring policy legacy q composes with.\"\n  (start {}))\n\n\n(defn outcome\n  \"The terminal outcome data, or nil while the state is live.\"\n  [state]\n  (::outcome state))\n\n\n(defn terminal? [state] (some? (::outcome state)))\n\n\n(defn steps [state] (::steps state))\n\n\n(defn phase [state] (::phase state))\n\n\n(defn- terminal-with\n  [state kind]\n  (assoc state ::outcome {:kind kind\n                          :phase (::phase state)\n                          :steps (::steps state)}))\n\n\n(defn charge\n  \"Consume the units of operation `op` (a key of `step-units`, or an\n   integer count). Refuses BEFORE performing the unit when no allowance\n   remains: the state turns terminal :step-budget-exhausted and the unit is\n   not consumed. No clock is read. A terminal state is returned unchanged.\"\n  [state op]\n  (let [n (if (integer? op) op (get step-units op))]\n    (when-not (and (integer? n) (pos? n))\n      (throw (ex-info \"unknown or non-positive step unit\" {:variable (str op)})))\n    (cond\n      (terminal? state) state\n      (and (some? (::remaining state)) (< (::remaining state) n))\n      (terminal-with state :step-budget-exhausted)\n      :else (cond-> (update state ::steps + n)\n              (some? (::remaining state)) (update ::remaining - n)))))\n\n\n(defn enter\n  \"Record a phase without observing the clock (the next step observes). A\n   terminal state is returned unchanged.\"\n  [state phase]\n  (if (terminal? state) state (assoc state ::phase phase)))\n\n\n(defn bounded?\n  \"True when the state carries a deadline or a work allowance.\"\n  [state]\n  (or (some? (::deadline state)) (some? (::remaining state))))\n\n\n(defn- observe\n  \"Observe the deadline without changing the phase. A terminal state is\n   returned as the SAME value and the driver is not touched.\"\n  [state driver]\n  (cond\n    (terminal? state) state\n    (nil? (::deadline state)) state\n    (>= (-observe driver) (::deadline state)) (terminal-with state :timeout)\n    :else state))\n\n\n(defn checkpoint\n  \"Observe the deadline at a named phase boundary. A terminal state is\n   absorbing: it is returned unchanged (equal as a whole value, phase\n   included) and the driver is not touched. Otherwise the phase is recorded\n   and, when a deadline exists, the driver is observed; expiry (observation\n   >= deadline) turns the state terminal :timeout.\"\n  [state driver phase]\n  (if (terminal? state)\n    state\n    (observe (assoc state ::phase phase) driver)))\n\n\n(defn step\n  \"One accountable unit, in the normative order: observe the deadline; if\n   live, charge the unit BEFORE it is performed (refusing when no\n   allowance remains). The caller performs the unit only when the returned\n   state is live, and observes again at its post-operation boundary. A\n   terminal state is returned unchanged, no clock read, no unit consumed.\"\n  [state driver op]\n  (let [state (observe state driver)]\n    (if (terminal? state) state (charge state op))))\n\n\n(defn read-step\n  \"Run ONE query-controlled storage read under the one-in-flight\n   guarantee. Observe before the read (a terminal or expired state starts\n   no read); run `read-fn` (indivisible: it may overshoot the deadline);\n   observe when it returns, before anything else can start another.\n   Returns [state value]. The value is ::not-read when no read started and\n   ::discarded when the post-read observation expired: a value whose\n   post-read observation expired is never delivered.\"\n  [state driver read-fn]\n  (let [state (checkpoint state driver :read)]\n    (if (terminal? state)\n      [state ::not-read]\n      (let [v (read-fn)\n            state (checkpoint state driver :read)]\n        (if (terminal? state)\n          [state ::discarded]\n          [state v])))))\n\n\n(defn run-reads\n  \"Perform `read-fns` in order, each through `read-step`, threading the\n   state. Stops at the first terminal state; later reads never start.\n   Returns [state values].\"\n  [state driver read-fns]\n  (reduce (fn [[st acc] f]\n            (let [[st v] (read-step st driver f)]\n              (if (terminal? st)\n                (reduced [st acc])\n                [st (conj acc v)])))\n          [state []]\n          read-fns))\n\n\n(defn finish\n  \"The final observation at the end of the pass. Exact-boundary completion\n   succeeds only when the state is live after it.\"\n  [state driver]\n  (checkpoint state driver :output))\n(defn- halted?\n  \"True once evaluation must stop: a terminal state, or a consumer that\n   answered `reduced` (first-witness termination).\"\n  [st acc]\n  (or (exec/terminal? st) (reduced? acc)))\n\n\n(defn- fold-st\n  \"Direct-style fold of f over xs, threading the execution state and an\n   accumulator: (f st acc x) -> [st acc]. Stops at a terminal state or a\n   reduced accumulator. A loop, so stack depth does not grow with the number\n   of items. Used where f is shallow (projection, pull, input expansion).\"\n  [st acc xs f]\n  (loop [st st\n         acc acc\n         xs (seq xs)]\n    (if (or (nil? xs) (halted? st acc))\n      [st acc]\n      (let [[st acc] (f st acc (first xs))] (recur st acc (next xs))))))\n\n\n;; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n;; The trampoline: explicit execution frames\n;; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n;; Evaluation is continuation-passing, and a continuation chain can grow with\n;; recursive rule expansion, nested evaluation and the bindings processed.\n;; Evaluator steps therefore never call each other (or their continuations)\n;; directly: a step returns a COMPUTATION, and `run-comp` is the only\n;; driver. A computation is a plain result [state acc], a bounce\n;; ({::bounce thunk}: call the thunk later) or a bind ({::m comp ::f f}: run\n;; comp, then hand its result to f). The pending fs are an explicit\n;; heap-allocated frame list, so host stack depth is constant however deep\n;; the evaluation nests, and a generous work allowance ends in structured\n;; exhaustion instead of a host stack overflow.\n\n(defn- bounce\n  \"Suspend thunk: it runs from the driver, after the stack has unwound.\"\n  [thunk]\n  {::bounce thunk})\n\n\n(defn- bind\n  \"The computation that runs m, then (f result).\"\n  [m f]\n  {::m m, ::f f})\n\n\n(defn- run-comp\n  \"The only driver: run a computation to its plain [state acc] result with a\n   constant host stack.\"\n  [c]\n  (loop [c c\n         frames ()]\n    (cond\n      (and (map? c) (contains? c ::f)) (recur (::m c) (conj frames (::f c)))\n      (and (map? c) (contains? c ::bounce)) (recur ((::bounce c)) frames)\n      (empty? frames) c\n      :else (recur ((first frames) c) (rest frames)))))\n\n\n(defn- each-st\n  \"Monadic fold of f over xs, threading the execution state and an\n   accumulator: (f st acc x) -> computation of [st acc]. Stops at a terminal\n   state or a reduced accumulator. Each item runs from the driver and the\n   rest of the fold is a frame, so the stack never grows with the number of\n   items or the nesting of what f evaluates.\"\n  [st acc xs f]\n  (let [xs (seq xs)]\n    (if (or (nil? xs) (halted? st acc))\n      [st acc]\n      (bind (bounce (fn [] (f st acc (first xs))))\n            (fn [[st acc]] (each-st st acc (next xs) f))))))\n\n\n(def ^:private unbounded-ctx {::driver nil})\n\n\n;; =============================================================================\n;; Realize a db-value: value tag -> {::relation ::fact-index ::indexes}\n;; =============================================================================\n\n(defn- relation->fact-index\n  [relation]\n  (index/index-datoms (mapv (fn [tuple]\n                              [(nth tuple 0) (nth tuple 1)\n                               (nth tuple 2) 0 datom/default-op])\n                            relation)))\n\n\n(defn- force-relation\n  \"Force a relation if it is delayed (e.g. from a lazy covered-index\n   realization) or return it directly. Memoization belongs to the delay:\n   forcing is therefore at most one walk, but this function itself neither\n   creates nor guarantees non-nilness.\"\n  [relation]\n  (if (delay? relation) @relation relation))\n\n\n(declare realize-st)\n\n\n(defn ^:no-doc realize-db-value!\n  \"Turn one db-value into an immutable tuple relation plus, for current\n   fact views, an in-memory fact index. Opens nothing, closes nothing.\n   Runs the checked realization with the explicit unbounded policy.\"\n  [db-value]\n  (second (realize-st exec/unbounded unbounded-ctx db-value)))\n\n\n(def ^:private raw-source-message\n  \"query db input must be a relation value, a datom view, or an opened published index; raw vectors and maps are rejected\")\n\n\n(defn- relation-size\n  \"The tuple count of a relation without forcing a deferred one.\"\n  [relation]\n  (if (delay? relation) 0 (count relation)))\n\n\n(defn- realize-view-st\n  \"Interpret a current/history view inside the query layer. The nested\n   source is another query value; a current view with no as-of over an\n   opened published index routes through the restored covered sets, every\n   other combination resolves the source's rows. The tuples interpreted and\n   indexed are charged before the indivisible host operation runs.\"\n  [st ctx d]\n  (let [kind (:dao.space.query/view d)\n        as-of (:as-of d)\n        [st src] (realize-st st ctx (:source d))]\n    (if (exec/terminal? st)\n      [st nil]\n      (let [{src-relation ::relation, src-indexes ::indexes} src]\n        (if (and (= :current kind) (nil? as-of) src-indexes)\n          [st {::relation (delay (d5->current-facts (force-relation src-relation) nil)),\n               ::fact-index src-indexes}]\n          (let [forced-rel (force-relation src-relation)\n                st (spend-n st ctx :scan-advance (relation-size forced-rel))]\n            (if (exec/terminal? st)\n              [st nil]\n              (let [rows (case kind\n                           :current (d5->current-facts forced-rel as-of)\n                           :history (d5->history-rows forced-rel as-of))\n                    st (spend-n st ctx :scan-advance\n                                (if (= :current kind) (count rows) 0))]\n                (if (exec/terminal? st)\n                  [st nil]\n                  (let [fact-index (when (= :current kind)\n                                     (relation->fact-index rows))\n                        st (observe-st st ctx)]\n                    (if (exec/terminal? st)\n                      [st nil]\n                      [st {::relation rows, ::fact-index fact-index}])))))))))))\n\n\n(defn- realize-st\n  \"The checked realization of one db-value: [state realized]. Tuples and\n   index entries are charged before the (indivisible) host construction and\n   the state is observed when it returns. A bounded state refuses an opened\n   published index: its traversal is not checkpointed.\"\n  [st ctx db-value]\n  (when-not (map? db-value)\n    (throw (ex-info raw-source-message {:input db-value})))\n  (cond\n    (contains? db-value :dao.space.query/relation)\n    (let [tuples (:dao.space.query/relation db-value)\n          fact? (:fact? db-value)\n          st (spend-n st ctx :scan-advance (if fact? (relation-size tuples) 0))]\n      (if (exec/terminal? st)\n        [st nil]\n        (let [fact-index (when fact? (relation->fact-index tuples))\n              st (observe-st st ctx)]\n          (if (exec/terminal? st)\n            [st nil]\n            [st {::relation tuples, ::fact-index fact-index}]))))\n\n    (contains? db-value :dao.space.query/published)\n    (do (when (exec/bounded? st)\n          (throw (ex-info \"bounded execution does not support opened published indexes\"\n                          {:reason :bounded-source-unqualified})))\n        (let [indexes (index/covered-indexes db-value)]\n          [st {::relation (:rows db-value),\n               ::indexes indexes,\n               ::fact-index nil}]))\n\n    (contains? db-value :dao.space.query/view)\n    (realize-view-st st ctx db-value)\n\n    :else\n    (throw (ex-info raw-source-message {:input db-value}))))\n\n\n;; =============================================================================\n;; match: Linda-style positional template (an ergonomic materializer)\n;; =============================================================================\n\n(def ^:private FREE ::free)\n\n\n(defn- wildcard?\n  [x]\n  (or (= x '_) (nil? x) (= x FREE)))\n\n\n(defn- query-var-symbol?\n  [x]\n  (and (symbol? x) (= \\? (first (name x)))))\n\n\n(defn- parse-tuple-pattern\n  \"Parse exact positional syntax plus an optional final `& tail`.\"\n  [pattern]\n  (let [pattern (vec pattern)\n        amps (vec (keep-indexed #(when (= '& %2) %1) pattern))]\n    (cond\n      (empty? amps) {:fixed pattern, :rest nil}\n      (or (not= 1 (count amps)) (not= (first amps) (- (count pattern) 2)))\n      (throw\n        (ex-info\n          \"Malformed tuple rest pattern: & must precede one final tail form\"\n          {:pattern pattern}))\n      :else (let [tail (peek pattern)]\n              (when-not (or (= tail '_) (query-var-symbol? tail))\n                (throw (ex-info\n                         \"Tuple rest pattern tail must be _ or a query variable\"\n                         {:pattern pattern, :tail tail})))\n              {:fixed (subvec pattern 0 (first amps)), :rest tail}))))\n\n\n(defn- tuple-shape-matches?\n  [{:keys [fixed rest]} tuple]\n  (if rest (<= (count fixed) (count tuple)) (= (count fixed) (count tuple))))\n\n\n(defn- slots-match?\n  [parsed tuple]\n  (and (tuple-shape-matches? parsed tuple)\n       (every? (fn [[expected actual]]\n                 (or (wildcard? expected) (cbor/content= expected actual)))\n               (map vector (:fixed parsed) tuple))))\n\n\n(defn- same-slot?\n  \"True while a range scan is still inside the slot value's index range:\n   compare-vals ties, not host =. The index orders by value, so 1 and 1.0\n   (or 1.0M and 1.00M) tie and interleave by the later slots; a scan that\n   stopped at the first kind-distinct row would miss the rows after it.\n   Kind-strict matching is the caller's filter, applied after the scan.\"\n  [expected actual]\n  (zero? (index/compare-vals expected actual)))\n\n\n(defn- range-scannable?\n  \"True for a bound slot value whose index range is exactly its kind-loose\n   equals: the scalar buckets of dao.space.index/compare-vals. A collection\n   or other value orders by host compare with a string fallback, which need\n   not agree with content= (a vector holding 1 and one holding the big\n   integer 1 are content= but print differently), so its matches are found\n   by scanning the other slots' range instead.\"\n  [x]\n  (or (nil? x) (boolean? x) (cbor/numeric? x) (string? x) (keyword? x) (symbol? x)))\n\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
